You work in the repo of team error404.ai for the Atira EHL Challenge ("From RFQ to bid"). Read CLAUDE.md, .entire/graph-agent.md, docs/plan.md and docs/learned.md first.

Follow all rules:
- fair play: no site values in code; must stay agentic (a model chooses where to go, what to search, what matters); helpers that parse, paginate, calculate, validate and fill forms are fine; several agents and parallel browsers are fine;
- git: author `josteng <jonas@stenglein.dev>`, imperative subject <= 50 chars, `-` bullet body, no Co-Authored-By or session links, never stage `.claude/`, never push;
- run `atira ... </dev/null` with timeouts >= 120 s;
- kill processes by PID only;
- prefer sandbox test runs.

Goal: drive down cost × time per run while keeping quality 100 reliable. Public ranking at equal quality is cost × time.
- Our main (95c6709): about $0.13-0.16 and 6-8 min (cost × time ≈ 50-75 $·s); about 300 model calls; 1.1-1.5M input tokens, ~50 % cached; 50-80k output.
- Leaders: #1 $0.024 in 1:38; #2 $0.092 in 3:20; #3 $0.108 in 3:31.
- Target: under $0.06 and under 4 min, without losing reliability. Reliability on the private dataset is still worth more than public rank, so never trade away a correctness check that matters.

Step 1, measure. Take 2-3 recent 100 runs on main, e.g. run_cb11bb57a6ac9d22, run_ae12f6d72b1cbbab and run_2423483796eed4e6. Get per-call LLM data: `atira` CLI / API, e.g. `GET /v1/runs/<id>/llm-calls` or whatever `atira --help` / the openapi at https://ehl-challenge.atira.ai/openapi.json offers (the MCP tool `run_llm_calls` maps to an API). Also check the run log's run summary (tracing.py prints one). Build a table per worker/phase:
- calls;
- input, cached and output tokens;
- thinking on/off;
- wall time;
- share of cost;
- whether it lies on the critical path.
Also check how the simulated Qwen price is computed: input vs cached vs output vs thinking tokens (`GET /v1/models/status`).

Step 2, cut the biggest items first. Candidates to evaluate (measure, don't guess):
- duplicate work: two parallel spec attempts (spec + spec2), verifier/dispute calls, `confirm_decision`, reader calls re-reading what a tool already parsed, summary checks, a planner call whose output barely varies;
- page views: `extract.py` view size, how many old views stay in the worker history (shrink older views harder), boilerplate (nav, footers, cookie text);
- thinking: off where the task is mechanical; shorter outputs, e.g. concise schemas and no free-text summaries nobody reads;
- workers that keep browsing after they have their answer: harness stops like `until_settled`; the Q&A worker is being handled by another agent, so skip it;
- critical path: what runs serially that could overlap, and the waits/settle times in `browser_tools.py`;
- step caps and retries that burn calls.

Keep it generic and agentic. Make each cut a separate commit, so we can revert one.

Step 3, verify:
- `cd agent && uv run --no-sync python -m unittest discover -s tests` must pass.
- Then 3 sandbox test runs: `git archive HEAD agent` into a temp dir under `$CLAUDE_JOB_DIR/tmp`, then `atira test-run ./agent --no-open </dev/null` with timeout 600 s.
- Record each with `python3 dev/track.py platform <run> --version v2 --experiment cost-cut --commit <sha> --dirty no --note "..."` from your worktree root, and commit the records.
- Report quality, cost, time and calls versus main.

Other agents are working in parallel:
- dead-page recovery in `browser_tools.py`, `submit.py` and `extract.py` bounds;
- the Q&A worker stop in `research_tender.py`.

Coordinate by keeping your edits small in those files. Report the measurement table, each cut with its measured saving, and the sandbox results. Commit on your worktree branch; do not merge.

